{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786209953,"nonce":"8/xYvC0Tmk9TIuvhhaA/CczvMsc=","title":"**What is wrong.** `POST /api/v1/channels/slack/interactions` verifies the Slack","message":"HMAC, parses the payload, and then throws it away. `internal/app/container.go:764`\nsets `Interactions: nil`, and `internal/channels/api/slack_interactions.go:100-110`\nonly calls `rt.interactions.Handle(...)` `if rt.interactions != nil`. Every\nauthentic interaction therefore falls through to `writeSlackAck(w)` — HTTP 200,\nempty body. Nothing is recorded, nothing is queued, no ack is written.\n\nThe comment in `container.go:761-763` is also wrong about its own behaviour: it\npromises the endpoint \"answers 503 rather than acknowledging work nobody will\ndo\". It answers 200.\n\n**Why it matters.** The Acknowledge button is the one thing a user is invited to\ndo from Slack, and it is on every card oto posts. Pressing it produces no error\nbanner, no reaction, no card update, and no state change in oto — the alert stays\nunacked in the UI. Silent success is the worst of the three possible outcomes: the\nuser believes the alert is handled. `docs/adr/0013` builds the whole\nalert-first scope story on ack being the single act oto asks of a human.\n\n**Where.** `internal/app/container.go:761-765`,\n`internal/channels/api/slack_interactions.go:100-110`,\n`internal/channels/api/ports.go` (the `Interactions` port), the ack path in\n`internal/alerts/service`.\n\n**Done when.** A block action on a real (or faked) Slack card reaches the alert\nack service, the alert shows `acked` with the Slack user resolved through\n`identity/repository/slack_identities.go`, the card is amended to reflect it, and\nan unroutable or unknown action is recorded rather than silently 200'd. A test\ndrives a signed block-action payload end to end and asserts the alert transitions.","files":null}]}